UnValList is designed to be run from a DLG Menu or from offline...

UnValList displays files that are waiting to be validated by the
sysop.

PLEASE READ:

   Before the contents of an area will be listed, the
user must have access to an auto-access file area, which
points to the validation holding area.  It is your auto-access
areas and the user's access to those areas, which determines
whether or not to display the contents of a validation holding
area.  For that reason, if you have a validation holding area
that is used by a private/non-autoaccess area and the same area
is pointed to by an auto-access area to which the user has
access, THE FILES WILL STILL BE LISTED.  You may need to create
new/additional validation holding areas if this "feature" is
not acceptable.

NOTE:  private/non-autoaccess areas have no effect on which
       validation holding areas are displayed.  (Which does not
       mean that a coresponding validation holding area will
       not be displayed, particularly if you have auto-access
       areas pointing to the same holding area.
       
(sorry for the redundancy, I just don't want any complaints
because of areas being listed that you don't want listed).

Just install it on a menu as follows:

[ 1] Letter:                   V
[ 2] Executable: (path/name)   DLG:Extra/UnValList
[ 4] Description:              Validation
[ 5] Type:                   **Executable
[ 6] Help File:                FILE/V
[ 7] Lower level:              1
[ 8] Upper level:              255
[ 9] Hidden:                   NO
[10] Load type:              **OVERLAY
[11] Ask user if sure:         NO
[12] Pend messages:            YES
[13] Cli mode:                 NO
[14] Pause on completion:      NO
[15] Log value:                None
[16] Activity string:          Viewing Files waiting to be validated...
[17] Priority:                 DEFAULT

Items marked with ** are important...

The path is not hard-coded.  Put it wherever you like and include
the appropriate path for item 2, above...  And of course you can
choose your own values for most of the other items...

CLI/NOCLI doesn't matter, and you can run it from TpTShell and/or a menuscript...

If you run it from offline, the list is printed without pausing (a
future version may allow page breaks if there's interest)...

Enjoy...

